{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Winter Twilight Photosynthesizers: Chlorobium in a Frozen Fjord",
    "date": "2026-09-06",
    "lesson": "In ice-covered polar fjords, green sulfur bacteria (Chlorobium) survive on almost no light at all — some of the lowest light intensities known to support photosynthesis on Earth. They live at the narrow depth where faint blue-green light filtering through meters of sea ice still barely reaches, and sulfide seeping up from anoxic sediments is still present. Because both resources are scarce and only overlap in a thin band, growth is slow and energy budgets are tight. This scenario shows anoxygenic photosynthesis running near its floor: light income barely exceeds maintenance cost, so the organism must bank energy efficiently and tolerate long dark stretches under ice and during the polar night.",
    "citation": "Marschall et al. 2010, Manske et al. 2005 (Black Sea Chlorobium); general green sulfur bacteria photophysiology at extreme low light",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Green sulfur bacteria (Chlorobium/Chlorobiaceae) surviving at extreme low-light depths beneath sea ice in high-latitude fjords, at the overlap of a faint light gradient and a sulfide plume"
  },
